Subject: register VM two-way property-read PIC with promotion

What this does:
  A register-VM property-read site is a single cached entry (`JSRegPIC`), and
  after `REG_PIC_MISS_LIMIT` (8) misses a site is marked dead and every read
  goes out of line to `regvm_get_field_ic` (~68 instructions) even when the
  site is 98% monomorphic and the inline hit is 22 instructions. On the fixed
  kernel the read sites are strongly bimorphic: of the sites with a live entry,
  1.13M see one shape, 3.0M see two, 475K see three, and 30K see four, so two
  ways cover 99.99% of the traffic.

  This gives each read site two ways. The register PIC and holder arrays are
  allocated at `2 * regvm_pic_count`; the primary way is still `pic[site]` and
  `holder[site]` (unchanged addressing), and the secondary is
  `pic[regvm_pic_count + site]` and its holder. The secondary is filled lazily,
  so a monomorphic site only pays the extra 24 bytes per table.

  Fill (`regvm_pic_fill`): when the primary already holds a different shape,
  install the new one in the secondary instead of giving up; the primary is
  left alive, so an alternating site keeps both hits inline and only a
  genuinely polymorphic site loses it. The secondary carries its own miss
  counter, so its own death (and the global-stub-cache fallback) is tracked
  separately from the primary's.

  Promote (`regvm_get_field_ic`): before the engine lookup and the global stub
  cache, probe the secondary. On a hit, serve it like any other borrowed read
  (so the quickened handlers are unchanged) and charge one promotion credit to
  the primary's `misses` field; at 16 credits the two ways and their holders
  swap wholesale, so the dominant shape becomes the one the 22-instruction
  inline hit serves. The threshold is 16 (64 was measured slower). The swap can
  change the primary's kind (OWN to PROTO); that is safe because every threaded
  caller requickens the instruction from `pic[site].kind` after
  `regvm_get_field_ic` returns, and a site belongs to exactly one instruction.

  All other walks of the table (free, GC mark, memory accounting) cover
  `2 * regvm_pic_count`; put sites use the primary indices only. The serialized
  bytecode still writes `regvm_pic_count` and rebuilds the table lazily on
  load, so the register format is unchanged.

  Measured (`fixed.js`, 32 iterations, i7-8700), on top of 0148: 23,244,145,368
  -> 23,101,044,263 instructions (**-0.143 G, -0.62 %**), against the plan's
  0.23 G alone (the 0.09 G gap is the overlap with 0148 -- both remove entries
  from the same chain sites, and the plan measured the pair at -0.73...-0.76 G).
  Cumulative base -> 0146..0149: 23,834,989,409 -> 23,067,899,335 (gate run),
  **-0.768 G**. Gate run 23,067,899,335 / 10,985,664,881. Checksum
  `546.1008391043414` exact; diff-suite `pass=398 fail=0 skip=0` mono and
  threaded; leak assertion clean; peak RSS 13200 KB; full test262 errors 50
  (<= 64, unchanged).

No opcode, operand encoding or serialized register-format change.
